In PL/I, the ERROR condition is triggered during input/output operations when an error occurs. You handle it using the ON ERROR statement with a GOTO or procedure call. Here's a practical example:

```pli
DCL FIL FILE RECORD;
DCL RECIN CHARACTER(80);
DCL EOF_FLAG BIT(1) INIT('0'B);
DCL ERROR_COUNT FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);

ON ERROR BEGIN;
  ERROR_COUNT = ERROR_COUNT + 1;
  IF ERROR_COUNT > 5 THEN DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('MAXIMUM ERROR COUNT EXCEEDED');
    CALL TERMINATE_PROCESSING();
  END;
  ELSE DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('ERROR OCCURRED: RETRYING...');
    GOTO RETRY_READ;
  END;
END;

OPEN FILE(FIL) INPUT SEQUENTIAL;

READ_LOOP:
DO WHILE(EOF_FLAG = '0'B);
  RETRY_READ:
  READ FILE(FIL) INTO(RECIN);
  PUT SKIP LIST('RECORD READ: ', RECIN);
END;

CLOSE FILE(FIL);
PUT SKIP LIST('PROCESSING COMPLETED SUCCESSFULLY');

TERMINATE_PROCESSING: PROCEDURE;
  IF FIL THEN CLOSE FILE(FIL);
  STOP;
END TERMINATE_PROCESSING;
```

Key points: ERROR condition fires on I/O failures (disk errors, write protection, etc.). Use GOTO for immediate retry logic. Implement error counters to prevent infinite loops. Always close files in error handlers. SIGNAL ERROR can manually trigger the handler for testing.

The ENDFILE condition in PL/I is raised automatically when a READ statement attempts to read past the end of a file. You handle it with ON ENDFILE. Here's a complete working example:

```pli
DCL INPUT_FILE FILE RECORD SEQUENTIAL;
DCL OUTPUT_FILE FILE RECORD SEQUENTIAL;
DCL RECORD_BUFFER CHARACTER(100);
DCL TOTAL_RECORDS FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);
DCL EOF_REACHED BIT(1) INIT('0'B);
DCL ERROR_MESSAGE CHARACTER(50);

ON ENDFILE(INPUT_FILE) BEGIN;
  PUT SKIP LIST('END OF INPUT FILE REACHED');
  PUT SKIP LIST('TOTAL RECORDS PROCESSED: ', TOTAL_RECORDS);
  EOF_REACHED = '1'B;
END;

ON ERROR(INPUT_FILE) BEGIN;
  PUT SKIP LIST('ERROR READING INPUT FILE');
  ERROR_MESSAGE = ONCODE();
  PUT SKIP LIST('ERROR CODE: ', ERROR_MESSAGE);
  STOP;
END;

OPEN FILE(INPUT_FILE) INPUT SEQUENTIAL;
OPEN FILE(OUTPUT_FILE) OUTPUT SEQUENTIAL;

DO WHILE(EOF_REACHED = '0'B);
  READ FILE(INPUT_FILE) INTO(RECORD_BUFFER);
  IF EOF_REACHED = '0'B THEN DO;
    TOTAL_RECORDS = TOTAL_RECORDS + 1;
    WRITE FILE(OUTPUT_FILE) FROM(RECORD_BUFFER);
    PUT SKIP LIST('RECORD ', TOTAL_RECORDS, ': PROCESSED');
  END;
END;

CLOSE FILE(INPUT_FILE);
CLOSE FILE(OUTPUT_FILE);
PUT SKIP LIST('FILE PROCESSING COMPLETE');
STOP;
```

Key points: ENDFILE automatically stops the DO WHILE loop when triggered. You can specify which file raises ENDFILE with ON ENDFILE(file_name). ENDFILE is different from ERROR - it's a normal condition, not an exception. Always check a flag before attempting more reads. Use ONCODE() to get specific error information.

The KEY condition in PL/I is raised when a READ operation with a key fails to find a matching record in an indexed sequential (ISAM) file. Here's a comprehensive example:

```pli
DCL ISAM_FILE FILE INDEXED SEQUENTIAL;
DCL KEY_VALUE CHARACTER(10);
DCL RECORD_BUFFER CHARACTER(200);
DCL RECORD_COUNT FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);
DCL KEY_NOT_FOUND FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);

ON KEY(ISAM_FILE) BEGIN;
  KEY_NOT_FOUND = KEY_NOT_FOUND + 1;
  PUT SKIP LIST('KEY NOT FOUND: ', KEY_VALUE);
  PUT SKIP LIST('SEARCHING FOR NEXT KEY...');
END;

ON ERROR(ISAM_FILE) BEGIN;
  PUT SKIP LIST('ISAM FILE ERROR DETECTED');
  PUT SKIP LIST('ERROR CODE: ', ONCODE());
  GOTO ERROR_CLEANUP;
END;

OPEN FILE(ISAM_FILE) INPUT;

DCL KEY_LIST(50) CHARACTER(10) STATIC INIT(
  'KEY00001', 'KEY00002', 'KEY00003', 'KEY00099', 'KEY00005'
);

DO I = 1 BY 1 UNTIL I > 5;
  KEY_VALUE = KEY_LIST(I);
  READ FILE(ISAM_FILE) KEY(KEY_VALUE) INTO(RECORD_BUFFER);
  IF ONCODE() = 0 THEN DO;
    RECORD_COUNT = RECORD_COUNT + 1;
    PUT SKIP LIST('FOUND: ', KEY_VALUE, ' - RECORD: ', RECORD_BUFFER);
  END;
  ELSE DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('FAILED LOOKUP FOR: ', KEY_VALUE);
  END;
END;

PUT SKIP LIST('TOTAL RECORDS FOUND: ', RECORD_COUNT);
PUT SKIP LIST('FAILED LOOKUPS: ', KEY_NOT_FOUND);

GOTO CLEANUP;

ERROR_CLEANUP:
  PUT SKIP LIST('HANDLING CRITICAL ERROR');

CLEANUP:
  CLOSE FILE(ISAM_FILE);
  STOP;
```

Key points: KEY condition is specific to indexed sequential (ISAM) files. KEY is raised when a READ with KEY() fails to find the record. Use ONCODE() to distinguish between KEY failures (non-zero) and successful reads. KEY is not an ERROR - it's a normal condition for missing records. Implement counters to track lookup failures for debugging. Always include proper cleanup in GOTO targets.

The SIGNAL statement in PL/I allows you to manually raise (trigger) conditions, enabling custom error flows and testing error handlers. Here's a detailed working example:

```pli
DCL FILE_HANDLE FILE RECORD SEQUENTIAL;
DCL VALIDATION_FAILED CONDITION;
DCL RECORD_BUFFER CHARACTER(256);
DCL RECORD_COUNT FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);
DCL RECORD_LIMIT FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(100);

ON VALIDATION_FAILED BEGIN;
  PUT SKIP LIST('VALIDATION ERROR TRIGGERED');
  PUT SKIP LIST('RECORD NUMBER: ', RECORD_COUNT);
  PUT SKIP LIST('RECORD CONTENT: ', RECORD_BUFFER);
  CALL LOG_ERROR('VALIDATION_FAILED');
  GOTO VALIDATION_RECOVERY;
END;

ON ERROR BEGIN;
  PUT SKIP LIST('SYSTEM ERROR DETECTED');
  SIGNAL VALIDATION_FAILED;
END;

ON ENDFILE(FILE_HANDLE) BEGIN;
  PUT SKIP LIST('EOF: SIGNALING COMPLETION');
  SIGNAL VALIDATION_FAILED;
END;

OPEN FILE(FILE_HANDLE) INPUT SEQUENTIAL;

READ_PROCESS:
DO WHILE(RECORD_COUNT < RECORD_LIMIT);
  READ FILE(FILE_HANDLE) INTO(RECORD_BUFFER);
  RECORD_COUNT = RECORD_COUNT + 1;
  IF RECORD_BUFFER = '' THEN DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('EMPTY RECORD AT: ', RECORD_COUNT);
    SIGNAL VALIDATION_FAILED;
  END;
  IF LENGTH(TRIM(RECORD_BUFFER)) > 200 THEN DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('RECORD TOO LONG AT: ', RECORD_COUNT);
    SIGNAL VALIDATION_FAILED;
  END;
  IF MOD(RECORD_COUNT, 10) = 0 THEN DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('MILESTONE: ', RECORD_COUNT, ' RECORDS PROCESSED');
  END;
END;

GOTO NORMAL_CLEANUP;

VALIDATION_RECOVERY:
  PUT SKIP LIST('RECOVERY IN PROGRESS');
  RECORD_COUNT = RECORD_COUNT + 1;
  IF RECORD_COUNT >= RECORD_LIMIT THEN DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('RECORD LIMIT REACHED');
    GOTO NORMAL_CLEANUP;
  END;
  ELSE DO;
    GOTO READ_PROCESS;
  END;

NORMAL_CLEANUP:
  CLOSE FILE(FILE_HANDLE);
  PUT SKIP LIST('PROCESSING COMPLETE: ', RECORD_COUNT, ' RECORDS');
  STOP;

LOG_ERROR: PROCEDURE(ERROR_TYPE);
  DCL ERROR_TYPE CHARACTER(*);
  PUT SKIP LIST('LOG: ERROR_TYPE=', ERROR_TYPE);
END LOG_ERROR;
```

Key points: SIGNAL triggers any condition (built-in or user-defined). You can define custom conditions and signal them for specific scenarios. SIGNAL can be used to flow control through error handlers strategically. SIGNAL within an ON handler creates cascading error handling. Use SIGNAL to test error paths without actual file I/O failures. Implement logging before SIGNAL to track error flow.

PLIRETC (PL/I Return Code) is a built-in variable that stores the most recent I/O operation return code. It's essential for error handling without relying solely on conditions. Here's a comprehensive working example:

```pli
DCL MASTER_FILE FILE RECORD INDEXED SEQUENTIAL;
DCL WORK_FILE FILE RECORD SEQUENTIAL;
DCL PLIRETC FIXED BINARY(31,0) EXTERNAL;
DCL CURRENT_KEY CHARACTER(10);
DCL FILE_RECORD CHARACTER(500);
DCL OPERATION_STATUS CHARACTER(20);
DCL SUCCESSFUL_OPS FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);
DCL FAILED_OPS FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);
DCL RETRY_COUNT FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(0);
DCL MAX_RETRIES FIXED BINARY(31,0) INIT(3);

ON ERROR BEGIN;
  PUT SKIP LIST('ERROR: PLIRETC = ', PLIRETC);
  CALL HANDLE_PLIRETC_ERROR();
  GOTO MAIN_RETRY;
END;

OPEN FILE(MASTER_FILE) INPUT;
OPEN FILE(WORK_FILE) OUTPUT;

PUT SKIP LIST('STARTING FILE OPERATIONS');

MAIN_RETRY:
  CURRENT_KEY = 'CUST0001';
  RETRY_COUNT = 0;

READ_ATTEMPT:
  IF RETRY_COUNT >= MAX_RETRIES THEN DO;
    FAILED_OPS = FAILED_OPS + 1;
    PUT SKIP LIST('MAX RETRIES EXCEEDED FOR KEY: ', CURRENT_KEY);
    GOTO NEXT_OPERATION;
  END;
  RETRY_COUNT = RETRY_COUNT + 1;
  READ FILE(MASTER_FILE) KEY(CURRENT_KEY) INTO(FILE_RECORD);
  IF PLIRETC = 0 THEN DO;
    SUCCESSFUL_OPS = SUCCESSFUL_OPS + 1;
    OPERATION_STATUS = 'SUCCESS';
    PUT SKIP LIST('READ SUCCESSFUL: KEY=', CURRENT_KEY, ' RC=', PLIRETC);
    WRITE FILE(WORK_FILE) FROM(FILE_RECORD);
    GOTO NEXT_OPERATION;
  END;
  ELSE IF PLIRETC = 1 THEN DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('KEY NOT FOUND: ', CURRENT_KEY, ' RC=', PLIRETC);
    FAILED_OPS = FAILED_OPS + 1;
    GOTO NEXT_OPERATION;
  END;
  ELSE IF PLIRETC = 2 THEN DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('FILE ERROR: RC=', PLIRETC, ' RETRYING...');
    GOTO READ_ATTEMPT;
  END;
  ELSE IF PLIRETC = 4 THEN DO;
    OPERATION_STATUS = 'EOF';
    PUT SKIP LIST('END OF FILE REACHED: RC=', PLIRETC);
    GOTO FINAL_STATS;
  END;
  ELSE DO;
    PUT SKIP LIST('UNKNOWN ERROR: RC=', PLIRETC);
    FAILED_OPS = FAILED_OPS + 1;
    GOTO NEXT_OPERATION;
  END;

NEXT_OPERATION:
  CURRENT_KEY = 'CUST0002';
  RETRY_COUNT = 0;
  READ FILE(MASTER_FILE) KEY(CURRENT_KEY) INTO(FILE_RECORD);
  IF PLIRETC = 0 THEN DO;
    SUCCESSFUL_OPS = SUCCESSFUL_OPS + 1;
    WRITE FILE(WORK_FILE) FROM(FILE_RECORD);
  END;
  ELSE DO;
    FAILED_OPS = FAILED_OPS + 1;
    PUT SKIP LIST('OPERATION FAILED: RC=', PLIRETC);
  END;

FINAL_STATS:
  CLOSE FILE(MASTER_FILE);
  CLOSE FILE(WORK_FILE);
  PUT SKIP LIST('FINAL STATISTICS:');
  PUT SKIP LIST('SUCCESSFUL OPERATIONS: ', SUCCESSFUL_OPS);
  PUT SKIP LIST('FAILED OPERATIONS: ', FAILED_OPS);
  PUT SKIP LIST('FINAL PLIRETC: ', PLIRETC);
  STOP;

HANDLE_PLIRETC_ERROR: PROCEDURE;
  SELECT(PLIRETC);
    WHEN(1) PUT SKIP LIST('RC 1: Record not found in file');
    WHEN(2) PUT SKIP LIST('RC 2: Permanent I/O error');
    WHEN(3) PUT SKIP LIST('RC 3: File not open');
    WHEN(4) PUT SKIP LIST('RC 4: End of file');
    WHEN(5) PUT SKIP LIST('RC 5: Record already exists');
    OTHERWISE PUT SKIP LIST('RC ', PLIRETC, ': Unknown error');
  END;
END HANDLE_PLIRETC_ERROR;
```

Key points: PLIRETC is set after EVERY I/O operation (READ, WRITE, OPEN, CLOSE). RC=0 means success; non-zero means various types of failure. Check PLIRETC immediately after I/O before any other statements. PLIRETC persists until the next I/O operation. Use PLIRETC with GOTO for precise control flow based on return codes. Common return codes: 1=not found, 2=I/O error, 4=EOF, 5=duplicate. Always implement retry logic with PLIRETC checks. PLIRETC is more reliable than relying solely on ON conditions.
